  "notes": "The funk-and-rock-facing crossover wing, and the entry is careful to say her kit is \"closer to a funk-band kit than to the behind-the-beat Soulquarians kit\" — \"live, loose and slightly loud, snare fat and on 2&4, tambourine and hand percussion doubling the backbeat, and a big open-hat/crash arrangement on the choruses\". That last clause is the one number that separates her from everyone else in this batch: `openHat.prob` 0.5 across three positions with a crash lane, where the rest of the lane sits under 0.25. The band is the catalogue's own 76–110, and the keys are the named C and B♭ majors against F minor, A♭ minor and B♭ minor. Harmony is the three families the entry writes out, with the triad weight raised for \"70s-funk-diatonic with dominant-7th and 9th colour\". The identifying melodic claim is stated as timbre rather than pitch — \"a heavily-rasped, nasal contralto delivered at low note-density in a narrow range, with the harmony doing the movement\" — so the register spans barely an octave and a half, density is 2–5, the intervals are almost entirely stepwise, and the movement is left to the chords. Bass is \"syncopated funk 16ths with slides and octave jumps, mixed loud\", hence the highest `glideProb` here and a register that reaches 48 with the chord voicing lifted to 50. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum probability and velocity band and all melody numbers; the research marks its drum-feel description as band-idiom inference. ⚠ The 128–188 readings the entry cites are doubled [LOW-CONF].",
